-- glslfx version 0.1

//
// Copyright (c) 2026 Nick Burkard
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
//
// usdGenHairStrandsMarschner.glslfx
//
// The second strand model of UsdGenHairStrands / UsdGenHairStrandsTranslucent,
// compiled in when the build selects it (CMake USDGEN_HAIR_SHADING_MODEL =
// marschner; usdGenHairModel.glslfx carries the choice). The default Karis
// model in usdGenHairStrandsBsdf.glslfx is untouched by it.
//
// WHAT THIS MODEL IS. An absorption-based Marschner model as real-time
// groom tools ship it, ported from the strand shader of the source groom tool,
// so an imported groom shades the way it did there. Compared with the Karis model:
//   * Lobe widths come from a Chiang-style longitudinal variance v:
//     B_R = 2 v Roughness (clamped to [1/255, 1]), B_TT = v/2, B_TRT = 2v,
//     standard deviations in sum-of-sines units, with no sqrt(2) cos(phi/2)
//     R scaling. Shifts are {-s, s/2, 1.5 s} for a cuticle shift s.
//   * Colour is an absorption coefficient: sigma from the albedo by Chiang's
//     inverse at azimuthal roughness 0.3, attenuated along the Marschner
//     path, exp(-2 sigma (1 + cos 2 gamma_t)) with gamma_t = asin(h / n'),
//     n' Bravais' index, TT's offset h a fitted polynomial in 1 - cos(phi/2).
//     F0 and n' follow `ior`.
//   * TT's azimuthal term is the model's 64 x 64 table, here in closed form
//     (UsdGenMarschnerTtAzimuthal); TRT's narrows with azimuthal roughness.
//   * Multiple scattering is Zinke dual scattering as the model composes it:
//     exp(-n) extinction of the direct BSDF plus a back-scatter lobe, a
//     forward-scatter term, everything times cos(theta_L), with the averages
//     read from its own table (UsdGenStrands.MarschnerTables).
//   * The sky is a flat, albedo-tinted ambient times exp(-k * local density)
//     hair occlusion, never the BSDF.
//
// Where it differs from the source, deliberately:
//   * The hair count n is usdGen's baked optical depth (furTauP/furTauN)
//     times selfShadow, shifted by one crossing like the Karis path, instead
//     of a per-frame density ray march. selfShadow 0.45 matches the source's
//     density normalisation (docs/storm-fur.md).
//   * Shading is per fragment, not per vertex, and the pixel-footprint
//     widening of the Karis path is applied to the direct lobes.
//   * Every light Storm hands the shader is evaluated, not one key light.
//   * The direct lobes read the shaded material's own shift, IOR and
//     azimuthal roughness; only the scattering table is built at the model's
//     defaults, as the source builds it from its first material.
//

--- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- glsl UsdGenStrands.MarschnerTables

#if defined(USDGEN_STRANDS_MODEL_MARSCHNER)
// ===========================================================================
// Hemispherical averages of the single-scattering model, for dual scattering.
//
// The model keeps them in a 32 x 16 x 16 half-float texture indexed by
// (|sin theta_L|, roughness, absorption coordinate) and filled on the GPU by
// Monte Carlo: per texel, the forward (cos(phi) < 0) and backward integrals
// a_f / a_b of S over solid angle, and the forward-half means of the R and
// TT azimuthal factors (no longitudinal term) the forward-scatter lobe
// reads. Stock Storm cannot bind a texture to a material terminal, so this is
// that texture recomputed by quadrature and resampled onto 12 incidence nodes
// (uniform in angle, denser toward grazing) x 5 roughness nodes x the 16
// absorption texels. Measured against the full lattice between nodes: a_f
// within 0.012 absolute, a_b within 0.003, the forward columns within 0.002.
// The texture's own half-float rounding is 0.0005.
//
// usdGenShaders/test/tabulate_hair_marschner.py builds the table, prints the
// block below and asserts (ctest testUsdGenHairMarschnerTable) that this file
// still holds it.
// ===========================================================================

// GENERATED by usdGenShaders/test/tabulate_hair_marschner.py -- do not
// edit by hand; re-run the script and paste its output.
